<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Ya'qubi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 897/898</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Ahmad al-Ya'qubi, Ibn Wadih al-Ya'qubi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Ya'qubi was an Arab historian and geographer of the Abbasid period who died in the late ninth century. He wrote a universal history and the Book of Countries, a geography based on his own travels and inquiries, which describes cities, routes and peoples from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. His work contains one of the earliest Arabic descriptions of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> kings.</p></div>
